[38;2;124;124;124mmultiplicity eigenvalues, and λ_i → 0.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe question is whether this is sufficient to conclude compactness.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor a self-adjoint operator: Yes, this is exactly the characterization. A self-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124madjoint operator on a separable Hilbert space is compact iff it has a complete [0m
[38;2;124;124;124morthonormal eigenbasis with eigenvalues converging to 0 and finite multiplicity.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mBut what if the operator is not self-adjoint? [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor a general (not necessarily self-adjoint) operator with a complete set of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124meigenvectors: If T has a complete orthonormal basis of eigenvectors {e_i} with [0m
[38;2;124;124;124meigenvalues {λ_i} and λ_i → 0, then T is the limit (in operator norm) of finite-[0m[38;2;124;124;124mrank operators T_n = Σ_{i=1}^{n} λ_i ⟨·, e_i⟩ e_i. The norm of T - T_n is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msup_{i>n} |λ_i| → 0. So T is a norm limit of finite-rank operators, hence [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcompact.[0m

